# Commit and push after user approval

Do not create a git commit or push unless the user has clearly asked for it in this conversation (for example: “commit”, “push”, “commit and push”, or “get this in”).

When they approve:

1. Commit only the work they approved. Do not add unrelated dirty files, secrets, or generated lockfile noise they did not ask to include.
2. Push to the **current** branch (`git push -u origin HEAD` if it has no upstream). Do not switch branches, force-push, or push to a different branch unless they name one.
3. Follow the repo git safety rules: no `--no-verify`, no amend unless their rules allow it, no force push to main/master.
4. After push, report the branch name, commit, and whether the remote was updated.

If they ask only to commit, do not push. If they ask only to push, do not invent a new commit.
